A||||7||87redGT||Intermittent Stalling||||8||
Z||000000||87redGT||11-13-2000||03:12 PM||||What could cause my intermittent stalling problem? Here's what's going on:<P>The engine pops and backfires then stalls usually when I'm idling at a stop light. However, on two occasions, while driving on the freeway, it just died!  It will not start right away, but if I wait for a few minutes, it usually starts, and then its fine again.<P>I checked for stored codes and found a 44.<P>The car's got 155+ K miles, and most of the sensors have been replaced within the last 3 years.  The fuel filter was replaced about a year ago, along with the cap, rotor, plugs, wire, ignition module, and ignition coil.<P>The battery's in good shape (showing 12 v on the dash voltmeter), and the only parts that I know to be bad are: the tach filter (which is disconnected), and maybe the oil sending unit because the oil gauge sometimes goes crazy during idle.<P>Help!!<BR><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||8
Z||000001||Phil||11-13-2000||03:51 PM|||| Your oil pressure sender also keeps your fuel pump running- might be something to check into. BTW it always helps to state what year and motor size||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||11-13-2000||10:32 PM||||97redGT, <BR>Check your cat, sounds like it's clogged.<P>Phil, <BR>The oil pressure only acts as a back up system if the fuel pump relay is fried.||||reg||1
Z||000003||strdream||11-13-2000||10:36 PM||||I believe acode 44 indicates a lean condition for a spacific Period of time. I would first suspect the ignition module. but as Phil suggests I would test fuel pressure first. After all it is the easyest test and can rule out low pressure as reason for the code. The ignition module can have a thermal brake down condition. It works untill it reaches a certain temp and than just quits, after it cools it works again. Code 44 is set based on information from the Oxygen sensor. A misfire due to ignition failure will send unburnt fuel into the exhaust, the O2 sensor doesn't recoginize the fuel, all it sees is the un-spent oxygen. The ECM trys to lean the mixture and is unsuccessful, still sees too much oxygen, sets a code.<BR>The reason I say check the module is your description of the shut down. It just doesn't sound like low fuel pressure to me.<BR>Ross<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||ROB MAC||11-14-2000||07:24 PM||||02 sensor can crap out in a year , as I found out. Don't think they will last ten years like the first ones.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||11-14-2000||07:42 PM||||look for vacuum leaks. Things that can move when the car is running. check all the EGR hardware, especialy if this is a v6.<P><BR>The oil pressure sender has known issues. Especially as it ages. I'd replace that just for insurance. (I will be replacing mine as soon as I get a few extra dollars just because its old.) This sender does have moving parts in it that do wear.<P>This could be a fuel problem or an ignition problem. Check all the connectors carfully for both systems. <P>Indiana resto,,,,, you might want to hang a meter on the ECM fuel pump control line and actually test your information. According to the ECM test sheet I have in front of me the ECM activates the fuel pump relay only for a couple seconds then shuts off. I've seen multiple instances now where one part of a GM manual says one thing and another part says something much differant. Also unless you have all the TSB's you don't have a full manual. The TSB's have all kinds of updates and changes to the original text. Even with all that I've seen manuals be wrong. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Rodrv6||11-14-2000||09:15 PM||||Ogre,<BR>Not shure about the V-6, but on the 84-86 dukes the fuel pump is operated continuously by the ECM whenever it sees distributer reference pulses indicating that the engine is turning over.  If all is well with the ECM, the engine will continue to run with the oil pressure switch disconnected.  I had my pressure switch removed completely for a while while I had a mechanical oil pressure guage installed to check on a possible oil pressure problem.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting :)||||reg||1
Z||000007||87redGT||11-15-2000||08:50 AM||||Thanks for all the help guys--I think my problem is fixed!  I replaced the fuel filter as well as the O2 sensor over the weekend, and was able to drive a full day yesterday without stalling.<P>I also replaced the faulty tach filter with one of Rodney Dickman's aftermarket replacement, which works well.<P>I still plan to check the fuel pressure as some of you suggested, just to be safe. And I also plan on checking the sending unit if it needs replacement--I heard that the oil/fuel sending unit from a 87 3.8 Bonneville will work with the 87GT for a fraction of the cost.  Any comments?<P>BTW, the ERG and ignition parts were o.k.<BR> <BR>Again, thanks for help friends!||||reg||14
